00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.790
at three drastically
different price points

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.540
to find out which one is the
most worth it at its price.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:39.530
- I'm excited.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.810
Bacon is a great food.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.970
- I'm not actually that
excited about this episode.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:44.803
- Really?

00:00:44.803 --> 00:00:45.679
- Yeah.

00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:46.512
It's just overrated.

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:49.700
- The smell and the sound
and the way it tastes.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.350
- I get it, but sometimes it's
a little bit of an overload.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:54.784
- I can definitely understand that.

00:00:54.784 --> 00:00:56.070
(sneeze)

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.880
- For this episode we
are going to be trying

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.730
three bacons not only
at three price points,

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.320
but in three different cities.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.153
Road trip.

00:01:03.153 --> 00:01:04.110
Sky trip.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.080
Home trip.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:05.913
- Yeah. Great.

00:01:05.913 --> 00:01:07.302
- We're going to Palm Springs, baby.

00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:09.340
(upbeat music)

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.850
- Hi, my name's Tara Lazar.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.490
I own Cheeky's.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.720
- [Andrew] How did Cheeky's come to be?

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.180
- I was sitting in front
of a computer day trading

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.090
and I really wanted a
place to eat breakfast.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.320
I'm from Palm Springs, so I thought

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.260
let's open a breakfast place.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.180
- You're kind of famous,
I guess, for you bacon.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.860
How did the bacon flight come to be?

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.570
- I like different kinds of bacon

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.440
and I didn't want to have to choose.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.370
When we do bacon flights, we
pick five different flavors.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.900
We change our flights every week.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.090
This week one of the
flavors is miso butter.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.100
Miso has a really nice salt content

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.280
so when you make bacon you
typically need a salt and a sweet

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.300
even if it's not gonna be a sweet bacon.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.150
The other one is rosemary sugar.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.470
That's fresh rosemary and chop it up

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.150
and just put some brown sugar and salt.

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.150
We bake almost all of our bacon.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.400
It's like the most even way

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.920
of creating a perfect slice of bacon.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.380
- Now what is your opinion
on crispy versus more tender?

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:57.700
- Such a crispy girl.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.533
How about you guys?

00:01:58.533 --> 00:01:59.990
- I do prefer a little bit of a chew.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.280
- I just don't want to fight my food.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.113
- Right.

00:02:02.113 --> 00:02:02.946
He wants it crispy.

00:02:02.946 --> 00:02:03.840
Easy to chew, easy to swallow.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.860
- Adam sometimes doesn't get
fed enough food on the show.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.080
- Should we try to make him something

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.032
that makes him feel special?

00:02:08.032 --> 00:02:09.320
- I think so.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.770
- We're kind of the breakfast expert, so.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:12.610
- Okay, why don't we leave that up to you?

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:13.443
- Okay.

00:02:13.443 --> 00:02:15.190
- I like that this feels like science.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:16.023
- Yeah.

00:02:16.023 --> 00:02:16.856
Cheers.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.370
- Tart but delicious.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.220
- [Steven] Bacon time.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.700
- I kind of like eating a
strip of bacon with my hand.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.533
Are you okay with that?
- Same.

00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:28.366
Yeah.

00:02:28.366 --> 00:02:29.199
- I have lots of cat
scratches on my hands, though.

00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:30.401
Is that okay Adam?

00:02:30.401 --> 00:02:31.234
- Wait, you have a cat?

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:33.256
- I don't, but I interact
with cats frequently.

00:02:33.256 --> 00:02:35.010
(laughter)

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:36.020
I found a cat.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:38.580
It won't be my friend.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:39.870
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.110
- Whoa.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.850
I've never had bacon like this before.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:46.683
- That is great.

00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:48.010
- It's neither chewy nor crispy.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.090
It's chewspy.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:52.882
It does taste like a warm bowl
of miso packed into bacon.

00:02:52.882 --> 00:02:54.370
That's a good appetizer.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:55.203
- Yes.

00:02:55.203 --> 00:02:56.036
- Let's bring out the next one.

00:02:56.036 --> 00:02:58.170
Rosemary bacon.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:02:59.240
Wow.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.090
Crispy outside.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.070
Chewy inside.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.040
There's pockets of fat drooling.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.032
My favorite thing is when foods drool

00:03:05.032 --> 00:03:07.620
because they realize how good they taste.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.650
Oh, oh no.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:12.830
- That's my favorite.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.250
Rosemary, top three herbs easily.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.989
- Holy crap, that was like the best slice

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:19.610
of bacon I've ever had.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:23.240
I can die happy now.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.100
- It's time to take flight
with the bacon flight.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.340
Beeler apple cinnamon, miso butter,

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.030
rosemary sugar, and a simple smoked one.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:32.230
Also, a jalapeno.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:33.698
This has got to be the way to go, right?

00:03:33.698 --> 00:03:36.000
- It's got to be the cheapest
flight you've ever taken.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:38.743
- Oh, man.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:40.750
That's the one.

00:03:41.749 --> 00:03:42.840
It's so good.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.960
It's like the best jalapeno kettle

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:46.610
cooked potato chip I've ever had.

00:03:47.661 --> 00:03:49.000
Cause it's bacon.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:51.523
- You full?
- I feel good.

00:03:51.523 --> 00:03:52.356
- Should we finish that one?

00:03:52.356 --> 00:03:53.189
- Sure.

00:03:53.189 --> 00:03:54.149
- Or give it to Adam?

00:03:54.149 --> 00:03:56.899
(cheerful music)

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.930
- That was an oh man on the Adam scale.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.263
- [Adam] Oh man.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.960
- Don't say we never feed Adam.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.210
- Can I get some of that?

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.340
(laughter)

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:10.363
Oh my god.

00:04:13.823 --> 00:04:14.997
(laughter)

00:04:14.997 --> 00:04:15.830
Alright.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:16.663
Back to L.A.?

00:04:16.663 --> 00:04:17.930
- In a minute.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.520
- The bacon flight.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.480
She's been doing it for 10 years.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.690
Where were you in 2007?

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:24.523
At the prom.

00:04:24.523 --> 00:04:25.356
- No telling.

00:04:25.356 --> 00:04:26.189
- Yeah, you were at prom.

00:04:26.189 --> 00:04:27.022
- I'm not telling.

00:04:27.022 --> 00:04:27.855
- You were dancing.
What were you

00:04:27.855 --> 00:04:28.688
doing 10 years ago?

00:04:28.688 --> 00:04:29.521
Playing RuneScape?

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.610
- Why as a matter of fact I was.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:34.490
- I pick Cheeky's.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.420
- I already picked Cheeky's.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.760
I really like Tara.

00:04:38.874 --> 00:04:40.194
(laughter)
What?

00:04:40.194 --> 00:04:41.860
Why are you laughing at that?

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:42.960
You didn't like Tara?

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.610
- Bacon fact.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.320
What we call Canadian bacon is actually

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.740
pork loin or back bacon, which means

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.830
that's it's technically
not the same as U.S. bacon,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.180
which is made from pork belly.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:03.350
- It makes sense cause Canada
is on the back of America.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:04.280
- Yeah.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.650
- American bacon, Canadian bacon.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:08.570
You're looking at the
complete pig right there.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:10.530
So now we're on our way to Manuela.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.800
- Getting something called a BLT.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.990
Bacon, lettuce, tomato.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:17.830
- It's a sandwich.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:19.540
- You know, I accept BLTs.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.350
- News flash, most people accept BLTs.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.900
Their main ingredient is bacon.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:27.440
- My name's Christian Truong.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.340
I'm the chef de cuisine at Manuela

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.693
restaurant in downtown L.A.

00:05:30.693 --> 00:05:32.670
- [Andrew] What sort of
restaurant is Manuela?

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:33.970
- Kind of Spanish flair.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.750
I wouldn't say Tex-Mex,
but along that line.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.320
We do local ingredients.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.200
Our menu changes daily depending on what

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.600
we get from the farmers market.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.540
- Today we're gonna be having your BLT.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.230
What are all the steps going
into delivering that dish?

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:47.760
- Getting the bread on the grill.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.130
Little char on it so you'll
have that char flavor.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.480
Mayo, tomato, and some greens.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.370
- The bacon you cure in house?

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:55.203
- Yes.

00:05:55.203 --> 00:05:56.036
- Curing is like this thing that

00:05:56.036 --> 00:05:58.490
I always hear happens to food.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.020
- You're pulling a lot of moisture out,

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.420
preserving that meat there.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.240
It changes the flavor, the texture.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.400
So when you cure it with
salt, sugar, maple syrup

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.290
for about five days then wash it off,

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:10.850
air dry it, and smoke it
for about three hours.

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.690
I think a BLT is just a simple
but really good sandwich.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.890
So in this case we want
to make the bacon shine.

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.730
- [Andrew] How much bacon
is going on this sandwich?

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.800
Is it like a pretty tall stack or what?

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:20.920
- [Christian] It's five slices.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.390
I think it's just the perfect amount.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:24.340
- So what do you have?

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.140
- I have the prettiest michelada

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:27.780
I've ever seen in my whole life.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.078
A little corny shone rammed into an olive.

00:06:30.078 --> 00:06:32.850
I'm gonna name a cat corny
shone someday in my life.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.640
- I've never been a big fan of micheladas.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.680
- It's a difficult taste to acquire.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:39.110
Like if tastes were
Pokemon, you would need

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:40.910
a master ball for the michelada.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:42.030
- Cheers.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.340
Fresh squeezed, fresh made.

00:06:46.118 --> 00:06:47.910
Oh yeah.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:51.570
That's some good, good juice.

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:52.680
- Let's have this sandwich.
- Okay.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.330
- Because I watched this thing

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:57.270
get made and I was blown away.

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:58.900
- Favorite part of that process?

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.500
Go.
- Easily the meat slicer.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:01.333
- Same.

00:07:01.333 --> 00:07:03.250
- Some people aspire to own luxury cars.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.740
You know, your Mercedes-Benz.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.240
I want to pay a premium to get

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.900
that hand cranked meat slicer.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:10.160
- Let's eat this BLT.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:14.500
Mm.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.600
I am sorry for all of the wrong

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.030
things I said about bacon today.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.470
Bacon is good and not overrated.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:22.040
- This bacon is at the perfect

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:25.060
point between crisp and tender.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:25.893
- Draw a graph.

00:07:25.893 --> 00:07:27.220
Crisp, tender.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.330
It's right on that line.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:31.620
You go off that line and
you look like a fool.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.480
- Pickle break.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:36.740
I love that pickle.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:37.790
That's a very good pickle.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:39.240
What is the shape of bacon?

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.230
It's like if you froze a weird sound wave.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:44.220
If a bacon strip was a sound wave,

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:45.574
what would it sound like?

00:07:45.574 --> 00:07:46.580
- Wa.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:48.340
No, you do it.

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.240
Do wa wa gu gu.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.480
It's wavy but not an even wavy.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.690
- You sound like a grandfather
talking to a child.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:57.220
What do you think about the bacon?

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:00.940
Bacon (speaking in foreign language).

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:02.630
It's Japanese for fact.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:05.400
In Germany, bacon is called speck.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.650
S-P-E-C-K.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:08.250
- That's a speck-tacular fact.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.409
- In Netherlands, bacon is called spek.

00:08:11.409 --> 00:08:13.040
But no C.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.240
- That's a spek-tacular fact.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.610
The French call it bacon or lard

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:21.530
and Italy considers
pancetta as we do bacon.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:24.588
So now we leave Los Angeles
to go to the last bacon spot

00:08:24.588 --> 00:08:28.930
right in New York City
at the Gansevoort Market.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:32.560
- Teleportation is real.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.560
- For our final price point
a multi course bacon menu.

00:08:38.108 --> 00:08:38.969
Omakase.

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:40.129
- Omakase?

00:08:40.129 --> 00:08:42.660
- Mama si, mama sa, omakase.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:45.460
(upbeat music)

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:47.350
- Welcome to Belly at Gansevoort Market.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:48.830
My name's Johnny Wooh.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:49.980
I'm the executive chef.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:52.430
Today you're gonna have bacon omakase.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:53.800
- And what is omakase?

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.420
- Omakase is literally
I leave it up to you.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.870
So I'll serve the best
food around the board.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.890
If you don't like it, I try
to like change it radically.

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:05.800
And if you like the course,
I just take the flow.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:07.450
- And what is your background?

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:09.310
- My background is a serious home cook.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:10.960
And this is exactly same setup.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.140
It's almost like serving to my friends.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:16.040
And, you know, after the
dinner you become my friend.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:16.873
- Very confident.

00:09:16.873 --> 00:09:17.910
I hope we're friends.

00:09:18.788 --> 00:09:20.740
- Beringer cabarnet.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:21.720
- Cheers.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.330
To the belly of the pig.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.460
- [Johnny] So the first course
of the day is bacon sushi.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:28.293
- [Steven] Ooh.

00:09:28.293 --> 00:09:29.471
I like where this is going.

00:09:29.471 --> 00:09:31.190
And what's on top here?

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:32.023
- [Johnny] Wasabi.

00:09:32.023 --> 00:09:32.856
- Cheers, Steven.

00:09:32.856 --> 00:09:33.689
- Cheers.

00:09:36.316 --> 00:09:37.390
- [Andrew] That's good stuff.
- That's delicious.

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:39.650
- I love seeing food get made.

00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:41.980
You start salivating in anticipation

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:43.270
and then there's no delay, right?

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.060
- It's the preview before the movies.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.780
- [Johnny] This is bacon carpaccio.

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:47.690
- [Steven] I'm gonna wrap it.

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.190
- I'm just going.

00:09:52.361 --> 00:09:53.321
- Oh.

00:09:53.321 --> 00:09:55.369
- What is that reaction?

00:09:55.369 --> 00:09:57.169
- We've had a lot of crunchy bacon,

00:09:57.169 --> 00:10:00.230
but this is nice and soft and luscious.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:01.880
That's so good.

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:02.980
Salad?

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:06.900
I did not sign up for a salad
but I will listen to the chef.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:08.840
- In Korea, mommies, grandma,

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.130
always make food with their hand.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:12.120
So that's what I'm doing.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.030
With love, you know?

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:14.720
We call it the hand taste.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:15.553
- I'll tell you what, though.

00:10:15.553 --> 00:10:18.290
My hands don't taste good
so don't get near my hands.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:19.123
- No problem.

00:10:19.123 --> 00:10:22.150
- [Steven] Oh.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:23.350
- [Andrew] Wow.

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:24.183
- Thank you.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:27.613
Mm.

00:10:27.613 --> 00:10:29.148
It's like a sesame vinegarette

00:10:29.148 --> 00:10:30.700
is what I would say it tastes like.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:31.600
You ready for bacon?

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:32.433
- Yes.

00:10:32.433 --> 00:10:33.266
- Cheers.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:36.493
Mm.

00:10:36.493 --> 00:10:37.350
- Ooh.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.040
It just had a little bit
of the mustard on its own.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:40.710
Mustard it super powerful.

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:43.660
- [Steven] I love how he
immediately read us and then left.

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:46.440
- [Andrew] He's like already
tinkering with something.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.239
I think I see a taco in my future.

00:10:49.239 --> 00:10:51.000
(laughter)

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.100
- Ma?

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:52.933
- It's brie.

00:10:52.933 --> 00:10:54.240
- [Andrew] It's brie cheese.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:55.668
- [Steven] Oh, yeah.

00:10:55.668 --> 00:10:58.610
(peaceful music)

00:10:58.610 --> 00:10:59.443
What is this?

00:10:59.443 --> 00:11:00.276
- Grapes.

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:04.530
- That's a really good combination.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.770
- The tortilla is actually dumpling skin.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:07.870
- Really?

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.610
- So it's a deconstructed
version of a dumpling.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:13.290
My mom, she ran restaurants
when I was young.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:16.220
I lived on the second
floor of that restaurant.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:19.512
I walk up by smelling food.

00:11:19.512 --> 00:11:20.921
I mean, it's in my blood I think.

00:11:20.921 --> 00:11:22.440
- Thank you.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:23.341
- Habanero.

00:11:23.341 --> 00:11:26.611
(peaceful music)

00:11:26.611 --> 00:11:27.501
- I needed this.

00:11:27.501 --> 00:11:28.334
- Yeah.

00:11:28.334 --> 00:11:30.910
- It was like bacon,
bacon, bacon, bacon, pasta.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:32.210
It's a good break to have.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:35.020
- Every time Adam eats I feel like

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:37.610
I'm watching a David
Attenborough documentary.

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.540
- This is a dumpling.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:40.373
- Okay.

00:11:40.373 --> 00:11:41.328
You ready?

00:11:41.328 --> 00:11:42.161
- I am.

00:11:45.204 --> 00:11:46.037
- Whoa.

00:11:46.037 --> 00:11:47.580
I just got this like wave of nostalgia.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:50.200
Like eating dumplings in my mom's kitchen.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:52.060
The chili is just like
kind of what she had, too.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:52.893
Wow.

00:11:52.893 --> 00:11:55.090
- So if we came again, would the menu

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:55.923
be different?
- It's a

00:11:55.923 --> 00:11:56.756
completely different menu.

00:11:56.756 --> 00:11:57.589
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:11:57.589 --> 00:11:58.422
- Do you remember that people come here

00:11:58.422 --> 00:11:59.443
and remember what you served them?

00:11:59.443 --> 00:12:00.340
- Of course.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:03.490
Sometimes I eat together,
drink together with customers.

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:04.420
- I'd love that.

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:05.253
Next time.

00:12:05.253 --> 00:12:06.086
2:01.

00:12:06.086 --> 00:12:07.010
- [Johnny] At 2:01, yes.

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:07.843
- Andrew.

00:12:07.843 --> 00:12:08.676
- Yes?

00:12:08.676 --> 00:12:10.250
- Three bacons, three price points.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:12.210
- Belly, you're in the
middle of this hectic market,

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.690
but somehow it's this
cozy at home experience.

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.440
But my Worth It winner is Manuela.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:19.670
They have a dedication to process

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.550
and it's a super cool place to eat.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:23.014
- Cheeky's is my Worth It winner.

00:12:23.014 --> 00:12:26.420
The bacon was five totally unique bacons.

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.120
Adam, what's your Worth It winner?

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:30.250
Can you believe that we've been

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.320
doing this for three seasons now?

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.530
- [Andrew] I've kind of
developed the diet of a bear.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:36.340
Force feeding every day and then

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:38.770
I go to sleep for six months
and I do it again and that

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:41.770
is the Worth It season.
- [Man] Oh yes.

